<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sangju</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sangju is a city in North Gyeongsang province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, on the upper Nakdong river. An ancient settlement and long one of the region's administrative centres, it gave half its name to Gyeongsang province, formed from the names of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a> and Sangju. It is now known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and dried persimmons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sangju.jpg" alt="Sangju" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Taken by Visviva and released into the public domain. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hamchangmound2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sangju lies on the north-western border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-gyeongsang-province">North Gyeongsang Province</a>, touching North Chungcheong Province. All of it lies in the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakdong-river">Nakdong River</a>, which several tributaries join, among them the Yeong, which rises in Mungyeong. The land slopes down from the Sobaek Mountains in the east to the river basin, and the highest point is the top of Songnisan, 1,058 metres above sea level. The terrain is hilly, with few flat areas along the rivers, and 67 per cent of the land is classed as vacant. The city spans about 49 kilometres from north to south and 43.3 kilometres from east to west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sangju first emerged after Silla conquered the area and subjugated small states such as Goryeong Gaya and Sabeol-guk. The dates are debated, but the area was under firm Silla control by 505. Under Silla the fortress of Sangju was one of the nine ju, or provincial units, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>, and it regulated traffic along the east-west route linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> valley, which Silla acquired in 553, with the Gyeongju area. Buddhist missionaries from Goguryeo travelling down from Haneuljae Pass in the 6th century would have reached it as their first major city.</p><p>In the late 9th century, as Unified Silla declined, rebellions broke out at Sangju. Ajagae seized the fortress and declared himself a general, then surrendered to Wang Kŏn in 918. The Silla noble Bak Eon-chang founded a kingdom of Sabeol-guk until the Later Baekje leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeon-hwon">Kyŏn Hwŏn</a> overthrew him in 927. Sangju became a county in 1895 and absorbed Hamchang County in 1914. Under Japanese rule the railway was completed in 1924, and an agricultural training institution that became Sangju National University was founded. The city and county were reunited on 1 January 1995.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sangju is traditionally agricultural, and about 40 per cent of its residents farm. It has the most farming households of any district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Silk production has been known here since Silla times, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> is the chief crop, with grapes, apples, pears, cucumbers and dried persimmons also grown. The city's nickname, Sam Baek or Three Whites, refers to rice, silkworm cocoons and dried persimmons. The Gyeongbuk Line railway and the Jungbu Naeryuk Expressway pass through it. In 2002 the first Carthusian monastery in Asia, the Charterhouse Our Lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, was founded in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sangju” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That text places it at Hamchang, near modern <b>Sangju</b> in North Gyeongsang, though some scholars doubt the identification.</blockquote><a href="/state/goryeong-gaya">Goryeong Gaya</a></li></ul></section>
